WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:11.600
This video is about adding extra information to maps in AvNav.

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:17.320
The technique for this is called "overlays." This means that additional data and

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:23.280
visualizations are overlaid on maps to display extra information

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:29.560
or to meaningfully expand the current map. Imagine you've

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:33.880
loaded a map of Germany, but you're sailing your boat

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:39.720
in the Flensburg Fjord, near the border between Germany and Denmark. This would force you

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:46.240
to constantly switch back and forth between a Danish and a German map, depending on your course and position.

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:53.000
To avoid this, you can add another map as an overlay in AvNav.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:01:00.040
This makes both maps visible in the navigation view, creating a seamless transition.

00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:05.960
You can also download information from other sources to

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:11.760
display a waypoint directory on the map, or, for example, a directory of Danish mooring buoys, and much more.

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:16.280
It's also possible to import routes and tracks onto the map

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:22.320
to compare them or use them as a template for creating a new route.

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:26.600
Satellite images can also be overlaid on AvNav maps,

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:32.240
but this requires a satellite image source and some preparation.

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:36.640
You can find this procedure in the documentation.

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:40.360
How are overlays assigned to specific maps?

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:45.720
To place two maps already in the system side by side, you can

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:53.440
use a very quick method: directly from the navigation page via the Charts button.

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:58.760
For one of the maps you want to place side by side, click the Overlay

00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:06.120
button on the right. This opens the Edit Overlay dialog. There, click the Insert

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:10.920
after button at the bottom to add another map to the base map.

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:15.800
Another dialog will then open; select the "chart" option and

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:23.040
click on the "name" field. Now you can choose from one of the installed maps.

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:27.760
After clicking OK, you will return to the initial "Edit Overlay" dialog and

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:34.800
confirm your new overlay with "Save." That's it! In the map list,

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:40.840
you can now identify the overlay by the blacked-out icon to the right of the map name.

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:47.240
To insert a route, waypoint list, or buoy directory as an overlay,

00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:51.680
you first need to download the corresponding files and

00:02:51.680 --> 00:03:00.800
import them into AvNav. AvNav accepts files in GPX, KML, KMZ, and GEOJSON formats.

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:06.840
After saving the source files to your computer, go to the

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:14.560
Charts/Overlays section in AvNav. In the Overlays column, you can then upload your files.

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:19.600
As an example, we'll use the waypoint list from NV-Verlag, which

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:24.600
is available for free download. Click Upload, select the file

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:29.040
, and check if it appears in the Overlay list.

00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:35.880
You could now follow the same steps described earlier for maps.

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:40.160
However, since you're already in the Charts/Overlays section, you can directly assign a file to a map from the

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:46.880
Overlay list—essentially the reverse process.

00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:52.720
To do this, click on the pencil icon next to the selected file, then click on Overlays and

00:03:52.720 --> 00:04:00.280
use the drop-down dialog to select the map to which you want to assign the overlay. They should,

00:04:00.280 --> 00:04:05.960
of course, be compatible. The buoys of the Danish Sailing Association will

00:04:05.960 --> 00:04:13.080
hardly be visible on a German or Dutch map… Once the overlay and map assignment is complete,

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:19.760
confirm with Save in the Edit Overlay dialog. In the map list on the navigation page,

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:25.680
you will now find the active overlay icon next to the map you just assigned.

00:04:26.920 --> 00:04:33.280
Incidentally, if you click on the pencil icon of an overlay in the Edit Overlay dialog, you can

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:39.760
adjust further settings for that overlay. Opacity can be

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:43.960
set in decimal increments from 0 to 1. It's best to enter the values ​​manually, e.g., 0.5

00:04:43.960 --> 00:04:49.040
for slightly translucent. Take your time to try out the different options

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:53.840
until you've developed a display that meets your needs.

00:04:54.760 --> 00:04:58.480
A little tip: The waypoint list only appears on the map

00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:05.600
when the text view is selected. Now both methods, such as maps and overlays, are available.

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:10.120
We've demonstrated how overlays can be combined and edited.

00:05:10.120 --> 00:05:14.960
A quick note at the end about working with overlays, for example, during a

00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:20.560
sailing trip. It can happen that the information superimposed by overlays

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:27.200
can interfere with the chart display. To quickly disable overlays, you can

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:33.440
do so directly on the navigation page via the Charts button and the displayed chart list

00:05:33.440 --> 00:05:39.600
by clicking on "Hide Overlays." Clicking "Show Overlays" will then

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:44.240
quickly reactivate the overlays for your selected chart. To avoid confusion

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:51.000
, only the currently selected option is active at any given time; the other is grayed out.

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:56.760
Now, have fun assigning overlays; the ability to combine adjacent

00:05:56.760 --> 00:06:00.720
charts is a great feature of AvNav! Thanks for watching!

